105	// tree hash names that content exactly, which is the whole premise of the cache
106	// key. This is what makes the relation verbs (neighbors/impact, which index the
107	// whole repository) warm on their second call instead of re-indexing from
108	// scratch — and it never hides a dirty edit, because a dirty indexable file
109	// fails the check and bypasses the cache exactly as before.
110	//
111	// "Indexable" is the load-bearing word. Requiring a wholly pristine tree made a
112	// stray untracked file that no parser ever opens — .DS_Store, a compiled binary,
113	// a log, an editor swap file — disable the cache for the entire repository, so
114	// every query re-indexed from scratch. A path that cannot contribute a symbol or
115	// a relation cannot make a snapshot stale either, so it is not a reason to throw
116	// one away.
117	//
118	// The test is extensionUnsupported, which is what the indexer uses to decide whether to PARSE a
119	// file — but parsing is not the only way a file reaches the graph. buildManifestImportResolver
120	// also reads the CONTENT of the repo-root manifests, and two of those (go.mod, setup.cfg) carry
121	// no supported extension while deciding how every import in the repository resolves. Forgiving
122	// them served a snapshot whose call edges were still resolved against the previous module path.
123	// They are excluded by name through isManifestImportFile, and TestManifestImportFilesCoverReads
124	// fails if that list falls behind the manifests actually read.
125	//
126	// Otherwise the rule is deliberately conservative in the safe direction: an extensionless path
127	// counts as supported, because a shebang can make it a script, and any supported path still
128	// bypasses the cache whatever its status.
129	//
130	// Ignored files never reach this loop: git status omits them, and the provider's walk skips them
131	// too, so neither side can serve what the other would have indexed. Note the two do not agree in
132	// general — git never ignores a TRACKED file, while the walk applies the ignore stack to every
133	// path — but that disagreement only ever reports a path as dirty that the walk would have
134	// skipped, which costs a re-index and cannot serve a stale one.
